% ch-04-ex-03-producer-consumer-naive-reverse.glp
%
% This file presents producer/2 + consumer/3 in their NATIVE chapter-4 home,
% byte-exact from book p 31 (PDF p 43), §4.2.1 + §4.2.2 "Producers and Consumers".
% These same procedures appear in ch03 ex-01 as a cross-chapter forward import
% (see olamni/tutorial/ch03/exercise-01/ch-03-ex-01-producer-consumer.glp); the
% byte-exact code corpus is identical, but the surrounding %% paraphrase context
% differs: ch03's header cites the cross-chapter import provenance, this file's
% header paraphrases the §4.2.1 + §4.2.2 native prose.
%
% Programs included (byte-exact):
%   §4.2.1 (book p 31): producer/2 — countdown stream from N
%   §4.2.2 (book p 31): consumer/3 — sums stream elements; uses Formal 4.2 SRSW-in-continuation-calls
%   §4.2.3 (book pp 31-32): naive reverse/2 + append/3 (O(n²))
%
% This is the FIRST of two .glp files in ex-03 per Clarifications Q4 spec
% amendment. The accumulator-based reverse/2 (book p 32) lives in the sibling
% file ch-04-ex-03-producer-consumer-acc-reverse.glp; the GLP non-contiguous-
% clauses constraint forces the split because both files define reverse/2.
%
% References Formal 4.2 (SRSW in Continuation Calls, book p 31).

%% §4.2.1 (book p 31): producer that counts down from N.

%% Producer base — when count is zero, the stream is the empty list.
producer([], 0).
%% Producer recursive — emit N at head, recurse on tail with N-1; built-in `>` guard ensures positive count; `:=` arithmetic body kernel decrements.
producer([N?|Xs?], N) :- N? > 0 | N1 := N? - 1, producer(Xs, N1?).

%% §4.2.2 (book p 31): consumer that sums stream elements.

%% Consumer base — when stream is empty, accumulator becomes the result via writer/reader pair Sum/Sum?.
consumer([], Sum, Sum?).
%% Consumer recursive — `ground(X?)` waits until head is fully bound; accumulator updated via `:=`; recursive call passes readers Xs?, Sum1? per Formal 4.2.
consumer([X|Xs], Sum, Result?) :- ground(X?) |
    Sum1 := Sum? + X?,
    consumer(Xs?, Sum1?, Result).

%% §4.2.3 (book pp 31-32): naive reverse/2 + append/3 (O(n²)).

%% Naive reverse base — empty list reverses to empty list.
reverse([], []).
%% Naive reverse recursive — recursively reverse the tail, then append the head as a singleton; O(n²) due to repeated appends.
reverse([X|Xs], Ys?) :-
    reverse(Xs?, Zs),
    append(Zs?, [X?], Ys).

%% Append base — appending an empty list yields the second list verbatim.
append([], Ys, Ys?).
%% Append recursive — emit head at output, recurse on tails.
append([X|Xs], Ys, [X?|Zs?]) :- append(Xs?, Ys?, Zs).
